{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CyGNYwWY7R9FAZuSKJx22T","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel01.2-uc001-sponge\n     2→title: \"Soak stdin and write to file via sponge\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs a pipeline that reads a file, transforms it, and pipes the result through\n     6→  the Go sponge binary back to the same file. sponge reads all of stdin before opening the\n     7→  output file, making the idiom \"cmd file | sponge file\" safe. The differential testing\n     8→  harness runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary (sponge from moreutils)\n     9→  with the same stdin and arguments, then compares the output file contents byte-for-byte.\n    10→  Unlike wc and cat, sponge verification uses file-content comparison, not stdout comparison.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd007-sponge.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce\n    16→  a passing differential test for cmd/sponge.\n    17→\n    18→flow:\n    19→  - id: F1\n    20→    step: |\n    21→      The operator runs a pipeline that pipes file content through the Go sponge binary back\n    22→      to the same file. Example: cat data.txt | ./bin/sponge data.txt\n    23→  - id: F2\n    24→    step: |\n    25→      cmd/sponge reads all bytes from stdin into an in-memory buffer. If the buffer exceeds\n    26→      the memory threshold, sponge spills to a temp file and continues reading. The output\n    27→      file is not opened during this phase.\n    28→  - id: F3\n    29→    step: |\n    30→      After stdin reaches EOF, cmd/sponge opens the output file. If the output file already\n    31→      exists, sponge reads its permissions for later restoration. In append mode (-a), sponge\n    32→      prepends the original file content to the buffered stdin content.\n    33→  - id: F4\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      cmd/sponge writes the buffered content to a temp file, then atomically renames the temp\n    36→      file to the output path. If the rename fails (cross-device), sponge falls back to a\n    37→      byte-for-byte copy.\n    38→  - id: F5\n    39→    step: |\n    40→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/sponge and the\n    41→      reference sponge binary with the same stdin, args, and environment in isolated temp\n    42→      directories.\n    43→  - id: F6\n    44→    step: |\n    45→      The harness compares the output file contents byte-for-byte using the file-state\n    46→      comparison capability (prd001-testutils R5). Both binaries must produce identical\n    47→      output file content.\n    48→  - id: F7\n    49→    step: |\n    50→      If the file contents match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    51→      triggering args, both file contents, and both exit codes.\n    52→\n    53→touchpoints:\n    54→  - T1: \"cmd/sponge — soak-before-write contract, atomic rename, append mode, passthrough (prd007-sponge R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n    55→  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, file-state comparison\"\n    56→\n    57→success_criteria:\n    58→  - id: S1\n    59→    criterion: cmd/sponge compiles and ./bin/sponge exists after mage build.\n    60→  - id: S2\n    61→    criterion: |\n    62→      The soak-before-write contract holds: \"cat file | ./bin/sponge file\" produces a file\n    63→      containing the original content (not empty or truncated), confirming sponge did not\n    64→      open the output file until stdin was fully consumed.\n    65→  - id: S3\n    66→    criterion: |\n    67→      Passthrough mode works: \"printf 'hello' | ./bin/sponge\" writes 'hello' to stdout,\n    68→      matching the reference binary.\n    69→  - id: S4\n    70→    criterion: |\n    71→      Append mode works: \"printf 'appended\\n' | ./bin/sponge -a existing.txt\" produces a\n    72→      file containing [original content][appended\\n], matching the reference binary.\n    73→  - id: S5\n    74→    criterion: |\n    75→      Large input (1 MB or more) triggers temp-file spill and produces correct output file\n    76→      content, matching the reference binary.\n    77→  - id: S6\n    78→    criterion: |\n    79→      File permissions are preserved: an output file with mode 0640 retains that mode after\n    80→      sponge overwrites it.\n    81→  - id: S7\n    82→    criterion: |\n    83→      The differential test passes for all modes: small stdin, large stdin, append, passthrough,\n    84→      and soak-before-write contract.\n    85→\n    86→out_of_scope:\n    87→  - Cross-device rename fallback is covered in the test suite but is not the primary flow.\n    88→  - SIGINT/SIGTERM cleanup behavior is specified in the PRD but is not directly testable in the\n    89→    differential harness; it is verified by manual inspection.\n    90→  - Memory threshold tuning is not tested; the spill path is exercised by the large-input test.\n    91→\n    92→test_suite: test-rel01.2\n    93→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UfZRom1wvEkoY4EpTGxoMu","session_id":"4bed78a5-ce10-4005-a086-7164c95aed1f","uuid":"7ba3d2c7-e612-4c28-81a9-ef33c07a3d2c"}
