n append mode and the file exists, I need to read the original content first before writing the new data. Still writing file operations...\n\nActually, I'm overcomplicating the temp file tracking. The cleaner approach is to always create an output temp file, write the original content to it if we're appending, then write the stdin data either directly or from the spill file, and finally handle cleanup of any separate spill file after the atomic rename succeeds.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the requirements—the PRD specifies that temp files go in TMPDIR and we rename to the output location, falling back to copy if they're on different devices. But I need to check whether I'm actually required to implement the full spill-to-temp-file behavior for stdin or if a simpler approach that satisfies the core contract is acceptable. The task does say to implement every requirement from the PRD, so I should go ahead with the complete implementation.\n\nFor tracking temp files, I'm going to keep it simple rather than maintaining a slice of registered files—I'll just track the spill file and output temp file separately as needed.\n\nLooking back at how the C sponge actually works, the signal handler will clean up both files. The implementation reads stdin into a buffer that grows as needed, spilling to a temp file when memory gets tight. Once stdin ends, it either writes directly to stdout or handles the output file case: checking the original file's permissions, handling append mode by prepending the original content, matching permissions on the temp file, then renaming it to the output path (or copying and deleting if the rename fails).\n\nNow I'm realizing the key pattern is that there's only one temp file at a time—it's created either during a spill or when writing to an output file. In append mode with a spill, the process chains together: read the original file, write it to a new temp, append the spilled stdin content, clean up the old temp, then rename. I think I'm overcomplic